{"authored" [{"question" "q-80454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the  first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$  and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-5)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6170015751e0b7d0dc814d683c342b9546097045b2986d01d259dbb522194c96" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3f9f987981e3d478b80a87779f8bafbb6f48e0c5dabc2a6eee1b46c476ddcd5" "basis" 2768 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076088/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-37085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-2)I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85620aa93903259d9b0ef76a6fa9e503ff1997e8da08edf68e6947a27f9440f8"} {"question" "q-37085" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37085" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37085" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85620aa93903259d9b0ef76a6fa9e503ff1997e8da08edf68e6947a27f9440f8"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$  and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$  and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the  first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85620aa93903259d9b0ef76a6fa9e503ff1997e8da08edf68e6947a27f9440f8"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37107" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-5)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85620aa93903259d9b0ef76a6fa9e503ff1997e8da08edf68e6947a27f9440f8"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$B$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-37633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85620aa93903259d9b0ef76a6fa9e503ff1997e8da08edf68e6947a27f9440f8"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"} {"question" "q-80454" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077175/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c28ce25052b8fc6e87a0386588c2983674a0b18bd1fc05255061dec9e74d273"}] "usage" {"q-37107" [] "q-37633" [] "q-80454" [] "q-37088" []} "basis" 2793 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186306235 17592186306236 17592186306237 17592186306238 17592186306239 17592186306249 17592186306250 17592186306251 17592186306252 17592186306253 17592186306323 17592186306324 17592186306325 17592186306326 17592186306327 17592186382660 17592186382661 17592186382662 17592186382663 17592186382664] "immutable_fields" [[17592186306234 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306234 :answer-field/id] [17592186306234 :answer-field/key] [17592186306234 :answer-field/type] [17592186306248 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306248 :answer-field/id] [17592186306248 :answer-field/key] [17592186306248 :answer-field/type] [17592186306322 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306322 :answer-field/id] [17592186306322 :answer-field/key] [17592186306322 :answer-field/type] [17592186382659 :answer-field/correct] [17592186382659 :answer-field/id] [17592186382659 :answer-field/key] [17592186382659 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
